│ is close to the healthy reference and nothing else is abnormal (latency is   │
│ not high while CPU is idle, the metrics show no errors). Go to Step 2A; no   │
│ investigation is needed.                                                     │
│   (B) SOMETHING ELSE: CPU per request is far from the healthy reference, OR  │
│ latency is high while CPU per pod is well below 0.75, OR you are not sure.   │
│ Go to Step 2B.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│ Step 2A - CAPACITY (SLO: latency p95 < 0.35 s | CPU throttling < 5% | CPU    │
│ per pod < 0.75 cores):                                                       │
│   - SLO violated by CPU pressure (CPU per pod near/above 0.75 or throttling  │
│ > 5%), OR throughput approaching/exceeding the safe limit of the current     │
│ replicas: scale UP with `scale_kubernetes_deployment(replicas=N)` to the     │
│ minimum replica count in the facts, and at least one more than now.          │
│   - SLO met and throughput significantly lower than the capacity of the      │
│ current replicas: you MUST scale DOWN to save resources, to the minimum      │
│ replica count in the facts.                                                  │
│   - SLO met and the current replica count already equals that minimum: no    │
│ action.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│ Step 2B - INVESTIGATE, then act on the cause. Look at ALL THREE observation  │
│ tools, one per step (the system refuses a restart or a rollback until you    │
│ have):                                                                       │
│   - `get_pod_breakdown()`: per pod: CPU, throttling, request rate, CPU cost  │
│ per request, p95, 5xx rate, event-loop lag, memory, restarts, age, revision. │
│   - `get_deployment_history()`: recent revisions of the deployment and what  │
│ changed in each.                                                             │
│   - `get_kubernetes_logs()`: warnings, errors and 5xx lines of every pod.    │
│   Then choose the action that addresses the cause, in this order:            │
│   - A change to the deployment shortly before the problem started            │
│ (deployment history): `rollback_kubernetes_deployment()` (previous revision) │
│ or `rollback_kubernetes_deployment(revision=N)`. A restart would recreate    │
│ the same bad configuration.                                                  │
│   - One pod clearly misbehaving while the others are healthy (errors, or     │
│ event-loop lag or CPU per request several times the other pods'):            │
│ `restart_kubernetes_pod(pod_name=...)`. Uneven CPU between pods under load   │
│ is normal load imbalance, not a fault.                                       │
│   - EVIDENCE of a bad state in the running processes: ERROR lines or a stuck │
│ / runaway job in the logs, or event-loop lag or CPU per request several      │
│ times the healthy reference: `restart_kubernetes_deployment()` (rolling, no  │
│ downtime). Warnings that only repeat the symptom (e.g. "High latency         │
│ detected") are not evidence of a cause.                                      │
│   - It is capacity after all: scale as in Step 2A.                           │
│   - No evidence of a cause and the SLO breach is small or short (e.g. right  │
│ after a restart or a scale, when new pods warm up): it may be a transient.   │
│ Answer "No action taken: <reason>"; the monitor wakes you again if it        │
│ persists.                                                                    │
│   More pods only help when CPU is the bottleneck. A restart only helps when  │
│ the bad state lives in the running processes. A rollback only helps when a   │
│ change caused it. If one of your recent actions failed verification, do not  │
│ repeat it without new evidence: the cause is probably something else.        │
│                                                                              │
│ Step 3 - EXECUTE exactly one remediation action, or none. The system         │
│ verifies it automatically afterwards and wakes you again if the problem      │
│ persists.                                                                    │
